<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Moldavian SSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1940 CE – 1991 CE · capital Chișinău</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet republic of 1940 to 1991, formed from Bessarabia, annexed from Romania, and part of the Moldavian ASSR; it became independent as Moldova in 1991.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Moldavian SSR was formed on 2 August 1940. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> had issued an ultimatum to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on 26 June 1940, and Soviet troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> on 28 June; the secret protocol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> had placed the province in the Soviet sphere. The new republic joined parts of Bessarabia with the six westernmost raions of the Moldavian Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontage went to the Ukrainian SSR.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> in 1941, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> reconquered it by the end of the war. Soviet authorities deported, imprisoned or executed many people: a Romanian commission report counted no fewer than 86,604 arrested and deported in 1940 and 1941, and 11,342 families were deported in two days in July 1949. Collectivisation in 1949 and 1950 followed a famine in which some sources give at least 115,000 deaths. Once Khrushchev replaced Stalin, deportees were allowed to return, and the republic received heavy investment. The economy was mainly agricultural, with most industry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transnistria">Transnistria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Supreme Soviet adopted the Moldovan language as official on 31 August 1989, written again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 23 June 1990. The republic was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Republic of Moldova</a> on 23 May 1991 and declared independence on 27 August 1991. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> ceased to exist on 26 December 1991, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/moldova">Moldova</a> was recognised. A civil war left a breakaway regime in control of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/transnistria">Transnistria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/moldavian-ssr.jpg" alt="Moldavian SSR" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: SVG by Pianist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Moldavian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1952–1990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1940 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chișinău</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Leonid Brezhnev, Ivan Bodiul</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Moldovan (Romanian written in Cyrillic), Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/moldova">Moldova</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Moldavian Soviet Socialist Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1966&amp;focus=moldavian-ssr" aria-label="Moldavian SSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5356 527 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Moldavian SSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5676 783L5820 533M5676 783L5676 783M5676 783L5696 788"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5820 533h0M5676 783h0M5696 788h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5676 783h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5676 783h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chișinău. <a href="/atlas?year=1966&amp;focus=moldavian-ssr"